namespace trion:
    use formal

    primitive moment

    section "A sentence is read as a contract":
        Composite vocabulary means the words of the trion language that join requirements in exactly one sentence.
        A simple sentence means a statement that holds no composite vocabulary.
        A composite sentence means a statement that holds composite vocabulary.
        The reading of a simple sentence is exactly one contract.
        The formal meaning of a specification is the conjunction of the contracts of the statements of the specification.

        [SENTENCE-ONE-CONTRACT]
        The trion processor shall read each simple sentence as exactly one contract.

        [SENTENCE-VERIFIABLE]
        The trion processor shall transform each contract into formulas.

        example SIMPLE-SENTENCE:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "the API shall store the message."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(API|store message) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|store message) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|store message))"

    section "Responsibility decides the assumption":
        An actor means a thing that a statement names as the subject of an action.
        A system declaration means a declaration that names an actor of a system.
        An environment declaration means a declaration that names an actor of the environment of a system.
        The system of a specification means the actors that the system declarations of the specification name.
        The environment of a specification means the actors of the specification outside the system of the specification.

        [SENTENCE-RESPONSIBILITY]
        The trion processor shall read each phrase that constrains the environment of a specification as a part of an assumption.

        [SENTENCE-GUARANTEE]
        The trion processor shall read each phrase that constrains the system of a specification as a part of a guarantee.

        An assumption sentence means a simple sentence that constrains only the environment of a specification.
        A guarantee sentence means a simple sentence that constrains the system of a specification.
        A descriptive sentence follows the responsibility of a normative sentence.

    section "Satisfiable is less than consistent":
        ref [SENTENCE-INPUT-ENABLED]
        ref [OBLIGATION-COMPATIBILITY]
        A normative sentence means a simple sentence that obliges, forbids, recommends, or permits.
        A descriptive sentence means a simple sentence that has no normative force.

        A sentence that requires the trigger of a contract excludes each response that avoids the trigger.

        example REQUIRED-TRIGGER:
            kind consistency
            input """
[A] When the API stores a message, the API shall notify the author.
[B] When the API stores a message, the API shall not notify the author.
[C] The API shall store a message.
"""
            expect inconsistent

    section "Formal vocabulary of contracts":
        ref [GRAMMAR-CONTRACT-MAPPING]
        ref [GRAMMAR-CONSTRUCTOR-ORIGIN]
        A contract atom means an atom of the assumption or of the guarantee of a contract.
        An act atom means a contract atom that states the completion of an action by a subject.
        A forbid atom means a contract atom that forbids an action.
        A discouraged atom means a contract atom that discourages an action.

        table "Atomic constructors":
            | Constructor | Signature | Meaning | Source rule |
            | --- | --- | --- | --- |
            | Act | `Act(action)` | The action occurs. The action key includes its actor and its stated arguments. | obligation |
            | Forbid | `Forbid(action)` | The action is forbidden. Negation means absence of a prohibition. | prohibition |
            | Discouraged | `Discouraged(action)` | The action is advised against. | recommendation-against |
            | Event | `Event(event)` | The named event occurs. A negative verbal clause negates its event. | clause-verbal |
            | Config | `Config(feature)` | The configuration includes the named feature. | frame-where |
            | Val | `Val(attribute, value)` | The attribute has the stated value. | predicate-words |
            | Dom | `Dom(attribute, value)` | The stated value belongs to the domain of the attribute. | predicate-enumeration |
            | Num | `Num(quantity, unit, comparison, bound)` | The value of the quantity in the given unit compares to the bound as specified. | predicate-comparison |
            | Lin | `Lin(terms, unit, comparison, bound)` | The sum of the quantities multiplied by their integer coefficients compares to the bound as specified. | relation |
            | Before | `Before(firstAction, secondAction)` | The first action completes before the second starts. | composition-sequence |
            | Rel | `Rel(relationName, individuals)` | The named predicate holds of the supplied individuals in their stated order. | description, existence |
            | Role | `Role(term, role)` | The term is bound to a named role in the specification. | doc-bind |

        table "Formula constructors":
            | Constructor | Signature | Meaning | Source rule |
            | --- | --- | --- | --- |
            | True | `True` | Holds in every interpretation. An absent local condition contributes True. | obligation |
            | Not | `Not(formula)` | Holds exactly when its formula does not hold. | obligation, prohibition |
            | And | `And(formulas)` | Holds exactly when all the formulas hold, including the empty conjunction. | obligation |
            | Or | `Or(formulas)` | Holds exactly when at least one formula holds. The empty disjunction is false. | actions-either |
            | Implies | `Implies(condition, response)` | Holds exactly when the condition is false or the response holds. | frame-if, condition-if-and-only-if, noun-phrase-none |

    section "A normative sentence and its contract":
        The modal verb of a normative sentence is "shall", "must", "should", or "may".
        The normative force of a normative sentence is obligation, prohibition, recommendation, or permission.
        An obligation means a normative sentence that requires an action.
        A prohibition means a normative sentence that forbids an action.
        A recommendation means a normative sentence that advises an action.
        A permission means a normative sentence that allows an action.

        The guarantee of an obligation holds an act atom, the negation of a forbid atom, and the negation of a discouraged atom.

        example OBLIGATION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The API shall stop."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(API|stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|stop))"

        The guarantee of a prohibition holds the negation of an act atom and a forbid atom.

        example PROHIBITION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The API shall not stop."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (¬Act(API|stop) ∧ Forbid(API|stop))"

        The guarantee of a recommendation holds the negation of a forbid atom and the negation of a discouraged atom.

        example RECOMMENDATION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The API should stop."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (¬Forbid(API|stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|stop))"

        The guarantee of a permission holds the negation of a forbid atom.

        example PERMISSION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The API may stop."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: ¬Forbid(API|stop)"

        An obligation of an action conflicts with a prohibition of the same action.

        example OBLIGATION-AGAINST-PROHIBITION:
            kind consistency
            input """
[A] The API shall stop.
[B] The API shall not stop.
"""
            expect inconsistent

    section "An environment condition is the assumption":
        A condition means a clause that restricts the situations of a simple sentence.
        Each phrase of a simple sentence belongs to the assumption or to the guarantee of the contract of the simple sentence.
        An environment condition means a condition that constrains only the environment of a specification.
        A system condition means a condition that constrains the system of a specification.
        Each environment condition belongs to the assumption of the contract of the simple sentence.
        Each system condition belongs to the guarantee of the contract of the simple sentence.

        The assumption holds at each occurrence of the event that a "When" clause names.

        example WHEN:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "When the door opens, the bell shall ring."
            expect "assume: Event(door open)"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(bell|ring) ∧ ¬Forbid(bell|ring) ∧ ¬Discouraged(bell|ring))"

        The assumption holds at each moment of the state that a "While" clause names.

        example WHILE:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "While the door is open, the lamp shall be lit."
            expect "assume: Val(door, open)"
            expect "guarantee: Val(lamp, lit)"

        An "If" clause names an unwanted event.

        example IF:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "If the sensor fails, then the alarm shall sound."
            expect "assume: Event(sensor fail)"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(alarm|sound) ∧ ¬Forbid(alarm|sound) ∧ ¬Discouraged(alarm|sound))"

        A "Where" clause names a feature that a system includes.
        Each "Where" clause is an environment condition.

        example WHERE:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "Where the car has a sunroof, the car shall have a sunroof control."
            expect "assume: Config(car have sunroof)"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(car|have sunroof control) ∧ ¬Forbid(car|have sunroof control) ∧ ¬Discouraged(car|have sunroof control))"

        A behavior satisfies each contract whose assumption does not hold in the behavior.

        example CONDITIONS-APART:
            kind consistency
            input """
[A] While the door is open, the lamp shall be lit.
[B] While the door is closed, the lamp shall not be lit.
"""
            expect consistent

    section "A descriptive sentence and its contract":
        A descriptive sentence has no modal verb.
        Each descriptive sentence is an assumption sentence or a guarantee sentence.
        A definition means a descriptive sentence that gives a meaning to a term.
        An enumeration means a descriptive sentence that lists the values of an attribute.

        example ENUMERATION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The door is open or closed."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (Dom(door, open) ∧ Dom(door, closed) ∧ (Val(door, open) ∨ Val(door, closed)))"

        example DEFINITION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "A guest means a user without an account."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (Dom(guest, user) ∧ (¬Act(guest|has account) ∧ ¬Act(guest|have account)))"

        An enumeration closes the values of an attribute.

        example VALUE-OUTSIDE:
            kind consistency
            input """
[D] The door is open or closed.
[A] The door shall be ajar.
"""
            expect inconsistent

        An enumeration assigns a listed value to the attribute.

        example VALUE-MISSING:
            kind consistency
            input """
[D] The door is open or closed.
[A] While the alarm is active, the door shall not be open.
[B] While the alarm is active, the door shall not be closed.
"""
            expect inconsistent

        example VALUE-LEFT:
            kind consistency
            input """
[D] The door is open or closed.
[A] While the alarm is active, the door shall not be open.
"""
            expect consistent
